   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law, in relation to adding \"embryo\" to the\ndefinition of \"tissue\" for purposes of tissue storage",
    "basePrintNo": "S3230",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3230-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 33,
       "fullName": "Gustavo Rivera",
       "imgName": "377_gustavo_rivera.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 377,
       "sessionMemberId": 2031,
       "sessionYear": 2023,
       "shortName": "RIVERA"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §4360, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S3230\n\nSPONSOR: HOYLMAN-SIGAL\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to adding \"embryo\" to\nthe definition of \"tissue\" for purposes of tissue storage\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends subdivision 10 of Section 4360 of the public health law\nto add \"embryo\" to the definition of tissues covered by the tissue bank\nand storage facility licensing law.\n\nSection 2 establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn vitro fertilization, commonly known as IVF, is an accepted and widely\nused method of assisted reproductive technology (ART). ART has helped an\nestimated six million couples in the United States who have trouble\ngetting pregnant or maintaining a pregnancy start families. IVF provides\na pathway to motherhood for women who have experienced life-threatening\ndiseases, such as cancer, and the reproductive damage that is often a\nconsequence of the treatments associated with these illnesses. It also\nprovides the same pathway for couples, regardless of sex or sexual\norientation, and is often a critical service to transgender individuals.\nIt is anticipated that by the end of the century, more than a million\nAmerican patients who suffer from some form of fertility issue will\nundergo IVF procedures.\n\nThe public interest in adequately protecting the human embryo tissue\nbank and storage facility industry is of the highest importance. Many\npopulations depend on these facilities to protect their ability to have\nchildren. The safe, effective regulation of embryo tissue banks and\nstorage facilities is critical to meeting this need. Under current law,\nembryos are not defined in the list of types of tissues that may be\nincluded in tissue bank and storage facilities. However, the New York\nState Department of Health includes embryos in their list of tissues\nthat they regulate. This bill would add embryos to the definition of\n\"tissue\" under Section 4360 of the Public Health Law, thereby confirming\nthe regulatory authority of the Department of Health in statute.\n\nAdditionally, Due to the increasing use of IVF and the advances in the\nscience of embryo storage, the Commissioner of Health should consider\nmodernizing and updating New York's regulations on embryo storage to\nincorporate new guidance including standards ISO 9001 and ISO 20387 of\nthe International Organization for Standardization, the standards for\nbiorepositories established by the International Society for Biological\nand Environmental Repositories and by the College of American Patholo-\ngists Biorepository Accreditation Program, and the FDA's guidance on\n\"current good tissue practices.\"\n\nThese standards are accepted best practices for protection of embryos,\nas well as for protection of the public interest in these important\nfacilities.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nS.0568 of 2021-2022 (Hoylman): Died in Health\n\nA.1135 of 2021-2022 (Paulin): Advanced to Third Reading Calendar\n\nS.5032-A of 2019-2020 (Hoylman): Died in Health\n\nA.7214 of 2019-2020 (Paulin): Died in Rules\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the state.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
