   "A": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law, in relation to factors for\nconsideration for applications for a license to operate a bank or storage\nfacility",
    "basePrintNo": "S3230",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3230-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 33,
       "fullName": "Gustavo Rivera",
       "imgName": "377_gustavo_rivera.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 377,
       "sessionMemberId": 2031,
       "sessionYear": 2023,
       "shortName": "RIVERA"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §4364, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S3230A\n\nSPONSOR: HOYLMAN-SIGAL\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to factors for\nconsideration for applications for a license to operate a bank or stor-\nage facility\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends section 4364 of the public health law to require appli-\ncants for a bank or facility license to demonstrate their ability to\nmoderate and records temperatures in their facilities, including an\nadequate alarm system; to establish a chain of custody for specimens;\nand to establish safeguards to protect patient records.\n\nSection 2 provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn vitro fertilization, commonly known as IVF, is an accepted and widely\nused method of assisted reproductive technology (ART). ART has helped an\nestimated six million couples in the United States who have trouble\ngetting pregnant or maintaining a pregnancy. IVF provides a pathway to\nmotherhood for women who have experienced life-threatening diseases,\nsuch as cancer, and the reproductive damage that is often a consequence\nof the treatments associated with these illnesses. It also provides the\nsame pathway for couples, regardless of sex or sexual orientation, and\nis often a critical service to transgender individuals. It is antic-\nipated that, by the end of the century, more than a million American\npatients who suffer from some form of fertility issue will undergo IVF\nprocedures.\n\nThe public interest in adequately protecting the human embryo tissue\nbank and storage facility industry is of the highest importance. Many\npopulations depend on these facilities to protect their ability to have\nchildren. The safe, effective regulation of embryo and tissue banks and\nstorage facilities is critical to meeting this need.\n\nThe New York State Department of Health includes embryos in the list of\ntissues that they regulate. This legislation adds standards that facili-\nties must demonstrate prior to licensure by the Department, including\nthe ability to moderate and record temperature and have an adequate\nalarm notification for unwanted temperature deviations, establishing a\nchain of custody for specimens, and creating safeguards to protect\npatient's records. The bill will address these important safety gaps and\nensure that DOH promulgates regulations to effect these standards.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nS.0568 of 2021-2022 (Hoylman): Died in Health\n\nA.1135 of 2021-2022 (Paulin): Advanced to Third Reading Calendar\n\nS.5032-A of 2019-2020 (Hoylman): Died in Health\n\nA.7214 of 2019-2020 (Paulin): Died in Rules\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the state.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law.\n",
